  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Gobius niger </em>occurs from the Canary Islands, to Mauritania and Senegal (Maugé 1986,&#160;Miller 1990, Eschmeyer 2013, Miller and Murdy in press).&#160;The records from Senegal are dubious and the known southern limit of the distribution for <em>G. niger</em> is Cape Blanc, Mauritania (Tito de Morias pers. comm. 2014).&#160;This species occurs in the Baltic Sea, the Mediterranean Sea, the North Sea, the Black Sea, the Suez Canal to Gulf of Suez, and the Red Sea (Maugé 1986,&#160;Miller 1986,&#160;Miller&#160;1990, Eschmeyer 2013, Miller and Murdy in press).&#160;<em>Gobius niger</em>is found between depths of 50-75 m (Miller 1990).",
    "population": "<em>Gobius niger </em>is common in museum collection records (260 lots). There is evidence of local abundance with some lots in museums containing over 50 specimens. The maximum number of specimens collected in one lot is 586&#160;(Accessed through the Fishnet 2 Portal, www.fishnet2.org, 2013-02-11). Vesey and Langford (1985) found <em>Gobius niger </em>to be the most common fish (533 individuals, 20.9% of catch) during a trawl survey of Stanswood Bay (England) from March 1983 to March 1984. The trawl survey consisted of two metre beam trawling near the shore and covered 800-1,000 m.&#160;Wheeler (1969) considered <em>Gobius niger </em>to be common in estuaries and low salinity coastal lagoons (see Vesey and Langford 1985).&#160;Magnhagen (1989) noted <em>Gobius niger </em>to be abundant in shallow soft-bottom areas along the Swedish coast.",
    "habitats": "<em>Gobius niger </em>is a coastal, benthic species that occurs in lower estuaries, lagoons, seagrass beds, over sand and mud bottoms (Wheeler 1969, Maugé 1986,&#160;Miller 1986).&#160;<em>Gobius niger </em>may live up to four or five years (Miller 1986, Magnhagen 1989). Miller (1986) noted&#160;<em>Gobius niger </em>reaches sexual maturity at two years, however, he did not specify whether this applies to males, females or both.&#160;Miller (1986) noted varying spawning periods throughout <em>Gobius niger </em>range: Naples (March-May), Veerse Meer, the Netherlands (April-early June), Baltic Sea (May-August), and Varna, Black Sea (April-September).&#160;In Stanswood Bay, England, <em>Gobius niger </em>spawns between April to the end of May with some fish retaining eggs and sperm until September. Egg counts ranged from 700 to 2,000 per female depending on size (Vesey and Langford 1985). Its&#160;diet&#160;consists of crustaceans such as larger amphipods, isopods, shrimps, mysids, small crabs, as well as bivalves, gastropods, polychaetes, chironomid larvae and sometimes small fish (Miller 1986).<strong><br/></strong><br/><br/><br/><strong><br/></strong>",
    "threats": "<em>Gobius niger&#160;</em>is uncommonly found in the aquarium trade, however, there are no known major threats to <em>Gobius niger</em>.",
    "measures": "There are no known species-specific conservation measures for <em>Gobius niger</em>.",
    "use_trade": "<em>Gobius niger </em>is uncommonly found in the aquarium trade. Otherwise, this species is not utilized.&#160;<strong><br/></strong><strong><br/></strong>",
    "rationale": "<em>Gobius niger</em>&#160;occurs in the Eastern Central Atlantic and is common in&#160;the Baltic Sea, the Mediterranean Sea, the North Sea, the Black Sea, the Suez Canal to Gulf of Suez and the Red Sea. It<em>&#160;</em>is common in museum collection records and there is evidence of local abundance. This species<em>&#160;</em>is uncommon in the aquarium trade.&#160;There are no known species-specific conservation measures for&#160;<em>Gobius niger</em>. Therefore, it<em>&#160;</em>is listed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
